Q) So basically  TensorRT-LLM is used for fine tuning and micro batching


Not quite! There's a common misconception here worth clearing up.
TensorRT-LLM is for Inference, not Fine-tuning
Task            What it means                                   TensorRT-LLM?
Pre-training    Training a model from scratch                   ❌ No
Fine-tuning     Further training on custom data                 ❌ No
Inference       Running a trained model to generate outputs     ✅ Yes


For fine-tuning, you'd use tools like HuggingFace Transformers, PEFT/LoRA, Unsloth, or NVIDIA NeMo
instead.

On "micro batching"
TensorRT-LLM does use a batching technique, but it's called in-flight batching (also called
continuous batching), not micro batching.

These are different things:

Micro batching — splitting a batch into smaller chunks during training to fit in memory
In-flight / continuous batching — dynamically adding new requests to an inference batch without waiting for others to finish, maximizing GPU utilization


So what is TensorRT-LLM actually for?
Think of the workflow like this:

    Fine-tune your model (HuggingFace, NeMo, etc.)
                ↓
    Optimize it for fast serving (TensorRT-LLM)
                ↓
    Deploy it to production (Triton Inference Server)

TensorRT-LLM sits at the "make inference as fast as possible" step — after training/fine-tuning
is already done.

In short: it's a serving/inference optimization tool, not a training or fine-tuning framework.


